The double shrine complex [[Hinokuma Shrine|Hinokuma Jingu and Kunikakasu Jingu]] holds two mirrors made before this one. Prototype mirrors {{ill||Higata no Kagami and Hiboko no Kagami|ja|日像鏡・日矛鏡}} respectively.

The researcher Shinsuke Takenaka said according to the legends, Amaterasu told {{ill|Ninigi-no-Mikoto|lt=Ninigi|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ninigi-no-Mikoto|12=draft|12=draft}}: "Serve this mirror as my soul, just as you would serve me, with clean mind and body."<ref name="bbctres"/><ref name="D-2012a">{{Cite web |last=D |first=John |date=2012-02-17 |title=The circular mirror: Shinto symbol |url=https://www.greenshinto.com/2012/02/18/the-circular-mirror-shinto-symbol/ |access-date=2023-12-12 |website=Green Shinto |language=en-GB}}</ref>

== Association with the structure of the Department of divinities ==
Yata no Kagami, alongside {{transliteration|ja|{{ill|Magatama#Yasakani no Magatama|lt=Yasakani no magatama|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Magatama#Yasakani no Magatama|12=draft|12=draft}}}} were heavily associated with Imperial ritual. Two of the main clans of the [[Department of Divinities]], the Kagamitsukuri, and the Tamatsukuri were descended from the creators of the respective artifacts: {{ill|Ishikori-dome no Mikoto|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ishikori-dome no Mikoto|12=draft|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Tamanooya-no-Mikoto|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Tamanooya-no-Mikoto|12=draft|12=draft}}. This was a part of a pattern of the clans of the Department of Divinities being descended from significant figures in the myth of Amaterasu in the cave.<ref name="hardacre-2017a">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_Q81DQAAQBAJ|title=Shinto: A History|last=Hardacre|first=Helen|date=2017|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=978-0-19-062171-1|language=en}}</ref>{{rp|35}}.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|59}} {{transliteration|ja|{{ill|Kusanagi no Tsurugi|lt=Kusanagi|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Kusanagi no Tsurugi|12=draft|12=draft}}}} is an exception, likely included to represent the submission of the [[Izumo clan]] to the [[Imperial House of Japan]].

In the [[Department of Divinities]] there was this organization.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}}.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}

* The {{ill|Inbe clan|Inbe family|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Inbe clan|12=draft|12=draft}} was responsible for managing the procuring materials for rituals and performing abstinence rites.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}} and distribution of tribute offerings within the Jingikan..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}} claiming descent from Ame no [[Futodama]] no Mikoto..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}
* {{ill|Sarume clan|lt=Sarume clan|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Sarume clan|en:draft|6=ja|7=猿女君|8=fr|9=Clan Sarume|12=draft|12=draft}} women were tasked with performing sacred dances..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}} Initially serving as seers and healers, their role evolved to include performing ritual dances, or kagura, during Enthronement Rites and court ceremonies..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}} claiming descent from the goddess [[Ama-no-Uzume|Ame no Uzume]] no Mikoto,<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}} and {{ill|Sarutahiko Ōkami|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Sarutahiko Ōkami|12=draft|12=draft}}. <ref name="picken-2004a">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=BKAhUVqKFhgC&q=Uzume&pg=PA1|title=Sourcebook in Shinto: Selected Documents|last=Picken|first=Stuart D. B.|date=2004|publisher=Greenwood Publishing Group|isbn=978-0-313-26432-0|language=en}}</ref><ref name="roberts-2009a">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xdfgjV2kw6oC&q=Uzume|title=Japanese Mythology A to Z|last=Roberts|first=Jeremy|date=2009|publisher=Infobase Publishing|isbn=978-1-4381-2802-3|language=en}}</ref><ref name="coulter-2013a">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=VWxekbhM1yEC&dq=Ame-no-Uzume&pg=PT91|title=Encyclopedia of Ancient Deities|last1=Coulter|first1=Charles Russell|last2=Turner|first2=Patricia|date=2013-07-04|publisher=Routledge|isbn=978-1-135-96397-2|language=en}}</ref><ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム">{{Cite web|title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細|url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9422|access-date=2024-03-18|website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム|language=ja}}</ref>
* The Tamatsukuri specialized in the creation of {{ill||Magatama|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Magatama}}..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}} and are descended from {{ill|Tamanooya-no-Mikoto|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Tamanooya-no-Mikoto|12=draft|12=draft}}. <ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|59}}
* The Kagamitsukuri were known for crafting {{ill|Mirrors in Shinto|lt=bronze mirrors|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Mirrors in Shinto|12=draft|12=draft}} for use in shrine ceremonies..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}} and are descended from {{ill|Ishikori-dome no Mikoto|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ishikori-dome no Mikoto|12=draft|12=draft}}. <ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|59}}
* The {{ill||Urabe clan|ja|吉田家|en:draft|Yoshida family|simple|User:Immanuelle/Yoshida family|fr|Clan Urabe}} (卜部氏) considered to be of relatively low Rank, carried out divination practices..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}}<ref>{{Cite web|title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細|url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/id=9910|access-date=2024-03-19|website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム|language=ja}}</ref> using tortoise shells and deer [[Scapula|scapulae]] <ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}} later taking the surname {{ill||Urabe clan|lt=Yoshida family|ja|吉田家|en:draft|Yoshida family|simple|User:Immanuelle/Yoshida family|fr|Clan Urabe}} and claiming descent from {{ill|Ame no Koyane|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ame no Koyane|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}} alongside the {{ill|Nakatomi clan|ja|中臣氏|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Nakatomi clan|fr|Clan Nakatomi|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}

* {{ill|Nakatomi clan|ja|中臣氏|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Nakatomi clan|fr|Clan Nakatomi|12=draft|12=draft}}: Later known as the {{ill|Fujiwara clan|lt=Fujiwara|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Fujiwara clan|12=draft|12=draft}}, they specialized in reciting prayers to the Kami, with a divine lineage tracing back to {{ill|Ame no Koyane|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ame no Koyane|12=draft|12=draft}} no Mikoto..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}
** In his book ''Jinja to kodai ōken saishi'' (1989). {{Interlanguage link multi|Iwao Ōwa|ja|大和岩雄|vertical-align=sup}} goes on to theorize that the {{Nihongo|{{ill|Ō clan|lt=Ō clan|ja|多氏|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ō clan|12=draft|12=draft}}|[[:ja:多氏|多氏]]|''Ō no uji''|extra=also written as {{Nihongo2|大氏}}}} was originally {{Nihongo|ōmi|大忌|"greater taboo (priesthood)"}}, but was usurped by the {{ill|Nakatomi clan|ja|中臣氏|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Nakatomi clan|fr|Clan Nakatomi|12=draft|12=draft}} who were among the "lesser priesthood" (the latter claims descent from the {{Nihongo|[[Inbe clan]]|[[:ja:忌部氏|忌部氏]]}}).<ref name="gakken2">{{cite book|title=神道の本|last=小向|first=正司|publisher=学研|year=1992|series=Books Esoterica|volume=2|pages=76–7}}(zasshi code 66951-07; kyōtsu zasshi code T10-66951-07-1000)</ref>
** Takemikazuchi was originally a local god (''kuni-tsu-kami'') revered by the {{Nihongo|{{ill|Ō clan|lt=Ō clan|ja|多氏|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ō clan|12=draft|12=draft}}|[[:ja:多氏|多氏]]|''Ō no uji''|extra=also written as {{Nihongo2|大氏}}}},<ref name="gakken2" /> and was a god of maritime travel.<ref name="gakken2" /> However, the {{ill|Nakatomi clan|ja|中臣氏|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Nakatomi clan|fr|Clan Nakatomi|12=draft|12=draft}} who also has roots in this region, and when they took over control of priestly duties from the Ō clan, they also instituted Takemikazuchi as the Nakatomi clan's [[ujigami]] (clan deity). Or so this is the observation by {{Interlanguage link multi|Iwao Ōwa|ja|大和岩雄|vertical-align=sup}}<ref name="gakken2" />

All of these clans are descended from figures who played significant roles in the {{ill||Tenson korin|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Tenson korin}} and accompanied Ninigi during his descent, and many of them ({{ill|Sarume clan|lt=Sarume clan|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Sarume clan|en:draft|6=ja|7=猿女君|8=fr|9=Clan Sarume|12=draft|12=draft}}, {{ill|Inbe clan|Inbe family|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Inbe clan|12=draft|12=draft}}, {{ill|Nakatomi clan|ja|中臣氏|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Nakatomi clan|fr|Clan Nakatomi|12=draft|12=draft}}, and {{ill||Urabe clan|ja|吉田家|en:draft|Yoshida family|simple|User:Immanuelle/Yoshida family|fr|Clan Urabe}}) descend from figures ([[Ama-no-Uzume|Ame no Uzume]], [[Futodama]], {{ill|Ame no Koyane|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ame no Koyane|12=draft|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Ame no Koyane|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Ame no Koyane|12=draft|12=draft}} respectively) played roles in the myth of Amaterasu in the cave.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|59}}

The {{ill||Mononobe clan|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Mononobe clan}} considered themselves excluded from this, and their ancestor {{ill||Nigihayahi|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Nigihayahi}} was excluded. For this reason they wrote the {{ill||Kujiki|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Kujiki}}. They were highly influential in the development of the department of divinities, but they were not a priestly clan. The Inbe felt their role was minimized so they wrote the {{ill||Kogo Shui|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Kogo Shui}} to improve their position too.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />

== Replicas in contemporary ritual ==
[[File:Masakaki with Mirror and Jewel.jpg|thumb|Right Masakaki at [[Yasaka Shrine]] featuring a Mirror and Magatama]]
[[File:Fukakusa Kaidoguchicho, Fushimi Ward, Kyoto, Kyoto Prefecture 612-0805, Japan - panoramio (1).jpg|thumb|A mirror on an altar at [[Fushimi Inari-taisha]], Yata-no-Kagami is seen as prototypical for the use of mirrors when worshipping other deities.<ref name="degaris-2013a" /><ref name="D-2012a" />]]
{{Main|Masakaki|en:Mirrors in Shinto}}
Replicas of the Imperial regalia of Japan including Yata no Kagami are included in {{ill|Masakaki|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Masakaki|12=draft|12=draft}} at Shinto shrines.<ref name="d-2015a2">{{Cite web |last=D |first=John |date=2015-04-05 |title=The Colours of Shinto (masakaki) |url=https://www.greenshinto.com/2015/04/05/the-colours-of-shinto/ |access-date=2023-11-27 |website=Green Shinto |language=en-GB}}</ref> Masakaki come in sets of two, with the left one containing a replica of {{ill|Kusanagi no Tsurugi|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Kusanagi no Tsurugi|12=draft|12=draft}} and the right one containing a replica of Yata no Kagami and {{ill|Yasakani-no-Magatama|8=en|simple|user:Immanuelle/Yasakani-no-Magatama|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref name="d-2015a2" /> {{ill|Mirrors in Shinto|8=en|simple|User:Immanuelle/Shinto mirror|12=draft|12=draft}} are highly significant with Yata no Kagami being seen as prototypical to them.<ref name="degaris-2013a">{{Cite book |last=De_Garis |first=Frederick |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=hUm0AAAAQBAJ&pg=PA237 |title=We Japanese |date=2013-09-05 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-136-18367-6 |pages=237 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="D-2012a" />

